^Location^

In the beginning, we explained that Finnish uses suffixes instead of prepositions. This is mostly the case, but there are still some prepositions that need to be a separate word. Most of these actually come _after_ the word, so they are more correctly called *post*positions. These need to be in the genitive. Here is a list of which ones come after a word:

*alla* (under)                           talon *alla* (under the house)
*takana* (behind)                    talon *takana* (behind the house)
*päällä* (on)                            talon *päällä* (on the house)
*edessä* (in front of)               talon *edessä* (in fron of the house)
*vieressä* (next to)                 talon *vieressä* (next to the house)
*jälkeen* (after)                       talon *jälkeen* (after the house)
*avulla* (with the help of)        talon *avulla* (with the help of the house)
*puolella* (on the side of)       talon *puolella* (on the house’s side)
*puolesta* (for)                        talon *puolesta* (for the house)

As you can see, the noun is given in the genitive form if there is a postposition. Below are the few prepositions that come _before_ the word, and these nouns instead use the partitive case:

*ennen* (before)          *ennen* iltaa (before the evening)
*ilman* (without)           *ilman* sinua (without you)

This last one can be used as both (but remember to use genitive/partitive):

*keskellä* (in the middle of)          tien *keskellä* / *keskellä* tietä (in the middle of the road)
*lähellä* (near)                              talon *lähellä* / *lähellä* taloa (near the house)


^Why would you use a separate word if there is also a suffix for it?^

It is true that many of these exist as suffixes (e.g. *päällä* and the *-lla/-llä* -ending both mean ‘on’) but suffixes tend to be more abstract and have many different meanings depending on the context and the word used. Prepositions on the other hand are more literal. 

Talolla on kissa. (There is a cat on the house / The house has a cat / At the house there is a cat)
Talon *päällä* on kissa. (There is a cat on the roof of the house)

Note also that the word *päällä* is actually the word *pää* (head) _with_ the *-llä* -ending! This is true for all preposition and postpositions, and thus they work as any normal word and can get any of the locative cases (but the meaning changes depending on if you use the internal or the external locative)

talon *päällä* (on the house)
talon *päältä* (from ‘on the house’)
talon *päälle* (onto the house)

talon *päässä* (at the end/corner of the house)
talon *päästä* (from the end/corner of the house)
talon *päähän* (to the end/corner of the house)

Some words such as *alla, takana, keskellä* cannot be used with the other locative version (e.g. *assa, takassa, keskessä* are wrong). There are far too many to list all the adpositions and their versions here, but you will learn the most used ones in context. If you want a list though, we recommend [this link to the Uusi Kielemme -website]

^Is there any shortcut to learn all of these?^

Sadly, no shortcut is going to work in your favor in the long run. The most effective way of learning anything difficult in Finnish is to use different learning methods at the same time, and especially using the language every day and encountering the difficult words often in different contexts. We choose to teach you the most common ones now, the less common ones later and leave out the least used ones, at least for now.